---
/**
 * Why a two-megabyte file can change a 115-million-parameter model: the correction has to pass
 * through a waist of eight dimensions.
 *
 * The animation is the argument. Both paths carry the same signal from the same input strip to
 * the same output strip; the top one goes straight through a 768 x 768 matrix, the bottom one is
 * forced through `r = 8` and back out. On the bottom path the dots are squeezed as they enter the
 * waist and open up again on the way out: the wire is thinner there and a funnel-shaped mask
 * clips the dots to a sliver while they cross it, so the motion shows the compression instead of
 * decorating it. Nothing about the drawing says the low-rank path is cheaper -- the counts do --
 * but the waist is what makes "cheaper" believable, and it is also exactly the limitation:
 * whatever the adapter learns has to fit through there.
 *
 * The mask lives on the group that holds the dots, not on the dots, so the CSS transform that
 * moves each dot (`fig-lora-travel`, in `global.css`) does not move the aperture. Under
 * `prefers-reduced-motion` the CSS parks one dot inside `W` and one inside the waist, where the
 * mask shows it squeezed.
 *
 * Numbers are `medium`'s, not an example: `d_model` 768, one query projection is 589 824 numbers,
 * its adapter is 12 288, and the whole adapter over sixteen layers is 393 216 of 115 120 128.
 *
 * Lives in a component rather than inline in the MDX for the reason the other figures do: MDX
 * wraps a multi-line JSX body in a `<p>`, and a `<p>` inside an `<svg>` ends the SVG early.
 */
const dots = [0, 1, 2, 3];
const LOW_Y = 227;
/** The funnel the mask opens for the bottom dots: full height until `A`, a sliver across the waist. */
const aperture = [
  [0, 180],
  [202, 180],
  [202, LOW_Y - 4.5],
  [232, LOW_Y - 1.2],
  [250, LOW_Y - 1.2],
  [280, LOW_Y - 4.5],
  [280, 180],
  [640, 180],
  [640, 262],
  [280, 262],
  [280, LOW_Y + 4.5],
  [250, LOW_Y + 1.2],
  [232, LOW_Y + 1.2],
  [202, LOW_Y + 4.5],
  [202, 262],
  [0, 262],
]
  .map(([px, py]) => `${px},${py}`)
  .join(' ');
---

<figure class="fig fig--lora" tabindex="0">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 640 300"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Dos caminos entre la misma entrada y la misma salida. El de arriba atraviesa una matriz cuadrada de 768 por 768 con 589 824 números. El de abajo pasa primero por una matriz de 768 por 8, se estrecha a ocho dimensiones en un cuello por el que la señal se aplasta, y se vuelve a abrir con otra de 8 por 768: 12 288 números en total, el 2,1 por ciento del primero."
  >
    <defs>
      <mask id="fig-lora-neck" maskUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x="0" y="180" width="640" height="82">
        <polygon points={aperture} fill="#fff"></polygon>
      </mask>
    </defs>
    <g font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-size="10" fill="var(--muted)">
      <!-- the input strip, shared by both paths -->
      <text x="14" y="24">
        entrada
      </text>
      <text x="14" y="40" font-size="9">
        768
      </text>
      <rect
        x="14"
        y="46"
        width="26"
        height="200"
        fill="var(--box-fill)"
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="1"
      ></rect>

      <!-- top path: the frozen weight -->
      <text x="150" y="26" fill="var(--ink)">
        W · congelada
      </text>
      <rect
        x="150"
        y="46"
        width="120"
        height="86"
        fill="var(--diagram-stroke)"
        fill-opacity="0.22"
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="1"
      ></rect>
      <text x="210" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink)">
        768 × 768
      </text>
      <text x="210" y="110" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5">
        589 824 números
      </text>
      <line
        class="wire"
        x1="40"
        y1="89"
        x2="150"
        y2="89"
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="1"
      ></line>
      <line
        class="wire"
        x1="270"
        y1="89"
        x2="560"
        y2="89"
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="1"
      ></line>

      <!-- bottom path: the adapter, through the waist -->
      <text x="150" y="176" fill="var(--accent-text-aa)">
        B · A · el adaptador
      </text>
      <rect
        x="150"
        y="192"
        width="52"
        height="70"
        fill="var(--accent)"
        fill-opacity="0.28"
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="1"
      ></rect>
      <text x="176" y="224" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--ink)">
        A
      </text>
      <text x="176" y="236" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">
        8 × 768
      </text>

      <!-- the funnels into and out of the waist: 768 wide at the matrices, 8 wide in between -->
      <polygon
        points={`202,192 232,${LOW_Y - 5} 232,${LOW_Y + 5} 202,262`}
        fill="var(--accent)"
        fill-opacity="0.1"
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="0.6"
      ></polygon>
      <polygon
        points={`250,${LOW_Y - 5} 280,192 280,262 250,${LOW_Y + 5}`}
        fill="var(--accent)"
        fill-opacity="0.1"
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="0.6"
      ></polygon>
      <rect
        x="232"
        y={LOW_Y - 5}
        width="18"
        height="10"
        fill="var(--accent)"
        fill-opacity="0.85"
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="1"
      ></rect>
      <text x="241" y="257" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--accent-text-aa)">
        r = 8
      </text>
      <text x="241" y="278" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9">
        768 → 8 → 768: la señal se aplasta y vuelve a abrirse
      </text>

      <rect
        x="280"
        y="192"
        width="52"
        height="70"
        fill="var(--accent)"
        fill-opacity="0.28"
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="1"
      ></rect>
      <text x="306" y="224" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--ink)">
        B
      </text>
      <text x="306" y="236" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">
        768 × 8
      </text>

      <line
        class="wire"
        x1="40"
        y1={LOW_Y}
        x2="150"
        y2={LOW_Y}
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="1"
      ></line>
      <!-- the cable through the waist is thinner: eight numbers, not 768 -->
      <line
        class="wire"
        x1="202"
        y1={LOW_Y}
        x2="232"
        y2={LOW_Y}
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="0.5"
      ></line>
      <line
        class="wire"
        x1="250"
        y1={LOW_Y}
        x2="280"
        y2={LOW_Y}
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="0.5"
      ></line>
      <line
        class="wire"
        x1="332"
        y1={LOW_Y}
        x2="560"
        y2={LOW_Y}
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="1"
      ></line>
      <text x="360" y="212" font-size="9.5">
        12 288 números · el 2,1 % de W
      </text>

      <!-- the sum, and the output strip -->
      <line
        x1="560"
        y1="89"
        x2="560"
        y2={LOW_Y}
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="1"
        stroke-dasharray="3 3"
      ></line>
      <text x="568" y="162" font-size="14" fill="var(--ink)">
        +
      </text>
      <rect
        x="586"
        y="46"
        width="26"
        height="200"
        fill="var(--box-fill)"
        stroke="var(--line)"
        stroke-width="1"
      ></rect>
      <text x="586" y="24">
        salida
      </text>
      <text x="586" y="40" font-size="9">
        768
      </text>

      <!-- the signal: the same dots on both paths, so only the waist is different -->
      {dots.map((index) => (
        <circle
          class={`fig-lora__dot fig-lora__dot--${index}`}
          cx="40"
          cy="89"
          r="3.5"
          fill="var(--ink)"
        />
      ))}
      <g mask="url(#fig-lora-neck)">
        {dots.map((index) => (
          <circle
            class={`fig-lora__dot fig-lora__dot--low fig-lora__dot--${index}`}
            cx="40"
            cy={LOW_Y}
            r="3.5"
            fill="var(--accent-text-aa)"
          />
        ))}
      </g>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption class="caption">
    La corrección no puede ser cualquier cosa: tiene que caber por ocho dimensiones, y en el cuello
    la señal se aplasta hasta pasar. Eso es lo que la abarata y también lo que la limita.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
